<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meknes</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Meknes is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, founded as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> settlement in the 10th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moulay-ismail">Moulay Ismail</a> made it his capital from 1672 to 1727 and built a vast imperial city of palaces, granaries, stables and walls, entered through gates such as Bab Mansour. Its historic city is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meknes.jpg" alt="Meknes" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Moleskine · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bab_Mansour_in_Meknes1732.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Meknes is one of the four imperial cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. It takes its name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miknasa">Miknasa</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribe whose name derives from a root meaning to fight. The Miknasa settled the region around the 10th century and founded small unfortified villages. After conquering the area in the 11th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> built a fortified settlement just south of them, called Tagrart, which formed the beginnings of the old medina. The Nejjarine Mosque and the Grand Mosque date from the Almoravid period.</p><p>Under the Alaouite sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-rashid-of-morocco">Moulay Rashid</a>, Fes was the capital, and his brother Moulay Isma'il governed Meknes. When Rashid died in 1672, Isma'il became sultan and chose Meknes as his capital. He had had to fight to reconquer Fes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> from his nephew Ahmad al-Mahriz, and the ulema of Fes disapproved of his ways, including his use of slaves. Meknes, further west, also seemed more defensible than Fes against Ottoman attacks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>.</p><p>His palace complex was built over his entire reign, from 1672 to 1727, on the site of the medieval kasbah, and covers an area about four times larger than the old city. It had several autonomous palaces, gardens, a water system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noria">norias</a>, canals and underground pipes, and monumental granaries said to hold supplies for a siege of ten years. After his death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> fell into relative anarchy as his sons competed for power, and Meknes lost its status as capital and was damaged by the earthquake of 1755.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The main monuments stand in the old medina and in the vast former kasbah of Moulay Isma'il to the south. Bab Mansur al-'Alj, the ceremonial entrance to the kasbah, was finished in 1732 by his son Moulay Abdallah. It is named after its designer, Mansour al-'Alj, a former Christian slave who converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and is decorated with carved brick and coloured zellij tilework. In front of it lies Place el-Hedim, whose name means rubble and recalls the demolitions Isma'il carried out while building his palaces.</p><p>After French rule began in 1912 a new city was built on the plateau opposite the old one, and the capital moved from Fes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a>. The Dar Jamaï Museum, in a palace of 1882, is the best-known museum, and the Roman ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volubilis">Volubilis</a> lie about half an hour to the north.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Meknes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the early 11th century that had been antithetical to Almohad doctrine: the Saffarin Madrasa of Fez in 1271 was the first, the al-Attarine followed in 1323, and the Bou Inania, the largest, was also a Friday mosque, with others from Salé and <b>Meknes</b> to Taza, Tlemcen and Marrakesh.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>An earthquake of estimated magnitude 6.5 to 7.0 devastated the cities of Fes and <b>Meknes</b> in Morocco, killing at least 15,000 people in both cities.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-27">27 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Moulay Ismail, ruling from 1672 to 1727, made <b>Meknes</b> a vast imperial city and built the Abid al-Bukhari, an army of enslaved black soldiers.</blockquote><a href="/state/alaouite-sultanate">Alaouite Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Its Berber forces defeated the Marabout state of al-Ayyashi in 1641, took Fez and <b>Meknes</b> and controlled the Republic of Salé.</blockquote><a href="/state/dilaiyya">Dila'iyya</a></li></ul></section>
